; core/bulk — resumable, flow-controlled bulk transfer (wire-mesh#34/#123).
; A genuinely new domain, not a composition of streaming.cddl +
; data-domain.cddl: stream-ack-frame's credit accounting is keyed by
; stream-session, whose own doc-comment scopes it to one connection, and
; reusing it for durable flow control would silently violate that
; documented contract; data-entries-frame's sequence space is keyed only
; by peer with durability explicitly "opportunistic" per its own comment —
; bulk transfer needs the opposite: a receiver's ack-seq is a hard
; durability guarantee, a materially different obligation from either.
;
; Two roles, two capabilities, matching the direction each manage-command
; actually flows: the sender opens a transfer (bulk.open, sender to
; receiver — "may I push this to you", gated by bulk:write) and the
; receiver drives resumption (bulk.resume, receiver to sender — "please
; continue sending from here", gated by bulk:read, since only the
; receiver's own durable state knows what is actually missing, the same
; reasoning data-request-frame's own receiver-driven design already
; follows). bulk.cancel may originate from either side, under whichever
; capability that side already holds for this transfer.
;
; seq/ack-seq are durable per transfer-id, not per-connection — a chunk
; keeps its seq no matter which connection eventually carries it, and
; bulk.resume on a fresh connection is the entire reconnection mechanism;
; connection-level reconnection itself needs no new machinery here, riding
; whatever ReconnectPolicy a session already uses.
;
; Resolved open questions (wire-mesh#34's own body left these open):
;
; - Chunk-size negotiation: deliberately NOT a wire field. Chunking
;   granularity is the sender's own implementation detail, the same as
;   stream-data-frame's own bytes field carries no size hint either — a
;   receiver's own resource limits are a deployment/implementation policy
;   question, not a protocol one, mirroring data-domain.cddl's own
;   "retention is policy, not protocol" precedent for the identical class
;   of question.
; - transfer-id/issuer pairing: transfer-id stays a bare, globally-unique
;   16-byte value, the same convention token-id/notice-id already use,
;   not namespaced by issuer in the CDDL shape itself. The real protection
;   is a verifier obligation instead (see below): a device-match check,
;   not a wire-level pairing field.
; - Token/capability expiry mid-transfer: no special mechanism needed.
;   bulk.open and bulk.resume are each independently, ordinarily
;   token-checked as manage-commands, exactly like every other verb in
;   this spec; bulk-data/bulk-ack/bulk-end frames are not manage-requests
;   and carry no per-frame token check of their own, the same way
;   stream-data-frame's own per-chunk frames carry none either — an
;   already-open transfer is not retroactively invalidated by its
;   opening token later expiring, only starting or resuming with an
;   already-expired one is refused.
;
; ack-seq counts chunks, not indices: it is the number of chunks durably
; persisted so far (equivalently, the seq the sender should send next),
; never "the highest index accepted" — the latter has no way to express
; "nothing received yet" under uint's non-negative constraint without an
; awkward sentinel, where the count-based reading makes ack-seq: 0 on the
; very first post-accept bulk-ack-frame mean exactly what it says.
;
; Verifier obligations:
;
; 1. A receiver MUST have durably persisted exactly the first ack-seq
;    chunks (seq 0 through ack-seq - 1) before advertising that ack-seq
;    in a bulk-ack-frame. An ack not backed by durable storage silently
;    loses data on the next resume, since the sender trusts it as "safe
;    to never retransmit below this point".
; 2. A sender MUST refuse a bulk.resume whose requesting peer device-id
;    does not match the device the corresponding bulk.open was originally
;    addressed to — this is the real protection the "transfer-id/issuer
;    pairing" open question above resolves to: without it, any other
;    equally-authorized peer under the same bulk:read scope could hijack
;    an in-flight transfer by learning or guessing its transfer-id.
; 3. bulk-end-frame's digest MUST be verified by the receiver against its
;    own durably-persisted chunks before treating the transfer as
;    genuinely complete; a mismatch means the transfer is corrupt, not
;    finished, regardless of what seq range was received.

transfer-id = bstr .size 16

bulk-open = {
  verb: "bulk.open",
  "transfer-id": transfer-id,
  ? "total-size": uint,
  ? "content-type": tstr,
  * tstr => any,
}
; ack-seq: where the receiver wants the sender to resume from (the count
; of chunks it has already durably persisted, per ack-seq's own
; count-based semantics above) — the entire reconnection mechanism; no
; separate bulk.open is sent again for an already-open transfer.
bulk-resume = { verb: "bulk.resume", "transfer-id": transfer-id, "ack-seq": uint, * tstr => any }
bulk-cancel = { verb: "bulk.cancel", "transfer-id": transfer-id, ? reason: tstr, * tstr => any }
$manage-command-params /= bulk-open / bulk-resume / bulk-cancel

; Chunk payload frames, riding the connection directly rather than
; manage-request-frame — the same "control travels as manage-command,
; bulk data travels as its own frame kind" split streaming.cddl already
; establishes for stream-data-frame vs. pty.spawn.
bulk-data-frame = { type: "bulk-data", "transfer-id": transfer-id, seq: uint, bytes: bstr }
; A receiver sends this proactively once it accepts a bulk.open (with
; ack-seq: 0, or its own already-known progress on a resumed transfer),
; giving the sender its initial credit window through the same mechanism
; used for every subsequent ack, rather than a one-off field duplicated
; into a bespoke bulk-open response shape.
bulk-ack-frame  = { type: "bulk-ack", "transfer-id": transfer-id, "ack-seq": uint, window: uint }
; Sent exactly once, by the sender, once every chunk of the underlying
; source has been transmitted — "the durable capture is fully delivered",
; deliberately independent of any other domain's own lifecycle frame
; (e.g. a core/exec stream-end-frame signalling "the process exited"),
; the same distinction the worked example in wire-mesh#34's own body
; draws between the two.
bulk-end-frame  = { type: "bulk-end", "transfer-id": transfer-id, digest: bstr }
